1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,880
Ancient philosophy is not just about talking or lecturing

2
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,710
or even reading long, dense books.

3
00:00:09,710 --> 00:00:14,790
In fact, it is something people have used throughout history -

4
00:00:14,790 --> 00:00:19,870
to solve their problems and to achieve their greatest triumphs.

5
00:00:19,870 --> 00:00:22,976
Specifically, I am referring to Stoicism,

6
00:00:22,976 --> 00:00:29,220
which, in my opinion, is the most practical of all philosophies

7
00:00:29,540 --> 00:00:31,260
and therefore the most appealing.

8
00:00:31,260 --> 00:00:39,464
Stoicism was founded in Ancient Greece by Zeno of Citium in the early 3rd century BC,

9
00:00:39,464 --> 00:00:46,130
but was practised by the likes of Epictetus, Cato, Seneca and Marcus Aurelius.

10
00:00:46,130 --> 00:00:49,650
Amazingly, we still have access to these ideas,

11
00:00:49,650 --> 00:00:55,810
despite the fact that the most famous Stoics never wrote anything down for publication.

12
00:00:55,810 --> 00:00:58,030
Cato definitely didn't.

13
00:00:58,030 --> 00:01:03,790
Marcus Aurelius never intended his Meditations to be anything but personal.

14
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:07,240
Seneca's letters were, well, letters

15
00:01:07,580 --> 00:01:13,490
and Epictetus' thoughts come to us by way of a note-taking student.

16
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:21,722
Stoic principles were based on the idea that its followers could have an unshakable happiness in this life

17
00:01:21,722 --> 00:01:25,380
and the key to achieving this was virtue.

18
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:28,260
The road to virtue, in turn

19
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:33,648
lay in understanding that destructive emotions, like anger and jealousy,

20
00:01:33,648 --> 00:01:40,160
are under our conscious control - they don't have to control us

21
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:42,950
because we can learn to control them.

22
00:01:42,950 --> 00:01:45,164
In the words of Epictetus:

23
00:01:45,164 --> 00:01:52,690
"external events I cannot control, but the choices I make with regard to them, I do control".

24
00:01:52,690 --> 00:02:01,489
The modern day philosopher and writer Nassim Nicholas Taleb defines a Stoic as someone

25
00:02:01,489 --> 00:02:04,300
who has a different perspective on experiences

26
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:11,544
which most of us would see as wholly negative;

27
00:02:11,544 --> 00:02:15,315
a Stoic "transforms fear into caution,

28
00:02:15,315 --> 00:02:21,600
pain into transformation, mistakes into initiation and desire into undertaking".

29
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:25,954
Using this definition as a model,

30
00:02:25,954 --> 00:02:27,680
we can see that throughout the centuries

31
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:38,291
Stoicism has been practised in more recent history by kings,

32
00:02:38,291 --> 00:02:41,920
presidents, artists, writers and entrepreneurs.

33
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:46,340
The founding fathers of the United States were inspired by the philosophy.

34
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:51,414
George Washington was introduced to Stoicism by his neighbours at age seventeen,

35
00:02:51,414 --> 00:02:58,180
and later, put on a play based on the life of Cato to inspire his men.

36
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:02,730
Thomas Jefferson kept a copy of Seneca beside his bed.

37
00:03:02,730 --> 00:03:08,150
Writers and artists have also been inspired by the stoics.

38
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:18,725
Eugène Delacroix, the renowned French Romantic artist (known best for his painting Liberty Leading the People) was an ardent Stoic,

39
00:03:18,725 --> 00:03:22,250
referring to it as his "consoling religion".

40
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:32,245
The economist Adam Smith's theories on capitalism were significantly influenced by the Stoicism that he studied as a schoolboy,

41
00:03:32,245 --> 00:03:36,980
under a teacher who had translated Marcus Aurelius' works.

42
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:40,480
Today's political leaders are no different

43
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:44,450
with many finding their inspiration from the ancient texts.

44
00:03:44,450 --> 00:03:50,267
Former US president Bill Clinton rereads Marcus Aurelius every single year,

45
00:03:50,267 --> 00:03:57,670
and many have compared former President Obama's calm leadership style to that of Cato.

46
00:03:57,670 --> 00:04:02,520
Wen Jiabao, the former prime minister of China

47
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:04,680
claims that Meditations is one of two books he travels

48
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:11,690
with and that he has read it more than one hundred times over the course of his life.

49
00:04:11,690 --> 00:04:16,640
Stoicism had a profound influence on Albert Ellis

50
00:04:17,020 --> 00:04:17,502
who invented Cognitive Behaviour Therapy,

51
00:04:17,502 --> 00:04:25,550
which is used to help people manage their problems by changing the way that they think and behave.

52
00:04:25,550 --> 00:04:29,440
It's most commonly used to treat depression.

53
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:39,130
The idea is that we can take control of our lives by challenging the irrational beliefs that create our faulty thinking,

54
00:04:39,130 --> 00:04:42,360
symptoms and behaviours by using logic instead.

55
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:47,200
Stoicism has also become popular in the world of business.

56
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:50,354
Stoic principles can build the resilience

57
00:04:50,354 --> 00:04:53,240
and state of mind required to overcome setbacks

58
00:04:53,820 --> 00:04:58,240
because Stoics teach turning obstacles into opportunity.

59
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:02,020
A lesson every business entrepreneur needs to learn.

60
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:09,711
I would argue that studying Stoicism is as relevant today as it was 2,000 years ago,

61
00:05:09,711 --> 00:05:15,140
thanks to its brilliant insights into how to lead a good life.

62
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:18,092
At the very root of the thinking,

63
00:05:18,092 --> 00:05:20,420
there is a very simple way of living

64
00:05:21,380 --> 00:05:25,260
control what you can and accept what you can't.

65
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:31,200
This is not as easy as it sounds and will require considerable practice

66
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:33,960
it can take a lifetime to master.

67
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:39,700
The Stoics also believed the most important foundation for a good

68
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:43,168
and happy life is not money,

69
00:05:43,168 --> 00:05:49,125
fame, power or pleasure, but having a disciplined and principled character -

70
00:05:49,125 --> 00:05:54,000
something which seems to resonate with many people today.
